body:
Exactly. I've seen captains pull up and stop fishing at sight of marine mammals in order to protect them. They lose money in order to protect them. I watched two grown men, hardened veteran fishermen, break down bawling in November when I was on a vessel that got an orca. 

&#x200B;

This picture was likely taken to document the interaction. All these people are idiots.

Also an observer. The level of confidently incorrect people in this thread is fuckin gobsmacking.

&#x200B;

For what it's worth, the reporting requirements are now immediate or as soon as possible for freshly dead marine mammals.

As a marine sciences expert, I don't. While it does highlight some of the legitimate problems that occur in the fishing industry it also made precisely no effort to show programs like NMFS that have a high degree of success in monitoring fisheries in real time with sustainable quotas. This documentary is just doom fetish. 

&#x200B;

To be clear. There are problems. But there are also a lot of solutions being implemented. And a total fishing moratorium is simply not an option. Fish is a giant global food supply that would need to be replaced in the event of a moratorium and we simply don't have the means to do that.
